      <h2 class="display section-title">The four numbers that matter most</h2>
      <p class="lede">Your report cherry-picks the worst readings for your zip, the same way the pros do. Here's what the four that spike highest actually mean, and how far past the safe limit they can climb where you live.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="edu-cards">
      <div class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-card-name">PM2.5 · Fine Particles</div>
        <p class="edu-card-plain">Smoke, exhaust, and combustion soot so fine it slips straight past your nose and settles deep in your lungs.</p>
        <div class="edu-stat"><span class="num">Up to 50×</span><span class="lbl">over the safe limit on a wildfire-smoke day</span></div>
        <p class="edu-why"><b>Why it matters:</b> these are the particles your lungs work hardest to clear out.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-card-name">PM10 · Coarse Particles</div>
        <p class="edu-card-plain">Road dust, construction grit, and pollen fragments kicked up into the air you breathe all day long.</p>
        <div class="edu-stat"><span class="num">Up to 20×</span><span class="lbl">over the limit during a dust storm</span></div>
        <p class="edu-why"><b>Why it matters:</b> enough of it leaves you clearing your throat without knowing why.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-card-name">Ozone · Ground-Level Smog</div>
        <p class="edu-card-plain">Not the good kind high up. This is the haze that forms when summer sun cooks traffic exhaust.</p>
        <div class="edu-stat"><span class="num">Up to 3×</span><span class="lbl">over the safe limit on a Code Red afternoon</span></div>
        <p class="edu-why"><b>Why it matters:</b> it's what makes a deep breath outside feel like work.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="edu-card">
        <div class="edu-card-name">Pollen & Mold · The Living Stuff</div>
        <p class="edu-card-plain">Tree, grass, and weed pollen plus mold spores that spike with the weather and the season.</p>
        <div class="edu-stat"><span class="num">Up to 12×</span><span class="lbl">over the "high" threshold on a spring morning</span></div>
        <p class="edu-why"><b>Why it matters:</b> your airways react to it every season, whether you notice or not.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="edu-source">Ranges show how far real-world readings can climb above U.S. <b>EPA Air Quality Index</b> safe thresholds during peak events like wildfire smoke, dust storms, and high-pollen mornings. Your free report shows the actual current numbers for your zip.</p>
    <div class="edu-invisible">
      <div class="edu-invisible-media"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline poster="https://bb-aq-v3-lander.pages.dev/assets/edu-particles-sunbeam.png"><source src="https://bb-aq-v3-lander.pages.dev/assets/particles-web.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div>
      <div class="edu-invisible-text">The scariest part isn't even on the chart. The particles small enough to reach your bloodstream are the ones you <b>can't see, smell, or taste.</b> PM2.5 is roughly 30 times smaller than a grain of sand. Your nose can't filter it. Your lungs are the last line of defense.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

